[[blenderpythonapi.pdf]]"],[13825,"[[blenderpythonapi.pdf]]"],[15520,"[[blenderpythonapi.pdf]]"],[17476,"[[blenderpythonapi.pdf]]"],[17584,"[[blenderpythonapi.pdf]]"],[17587,"[[blenderpythonapi.pdf]]"],[17590,"[[blenderpythonapi.pdf]]"],[17593,"[[blenderpythonapi.pdf]]"],[17686,"[[blenderpythonapi.pdf]]"],[17689,"[[blenderpythonapi.pdf]]"],[17692,"[[blenderpythonapi.pdf]]"],[17749,"[[blenderpythonapi.pdf]]"],[17758,"[[blenderpythonapi.pdf]]"],[17794,"[[blenderpythonapi.pdf]]"],[17803,"[[blenderpythonapi.pdf]]"],[17872,"[[blenderpythonapi.pdf]]"],[17941,"[[blenderpythonapi.pdf]]"],[17968,"[[blenderpythonapi.pdf]]"],[17980,"[[blenderpythonapi.pdf]]"],[17998,"[[blenderpythonapi.pdf]]"],[18031,"[[blenderpythonapi.pdf]]"],[18049,"[[blenderpythonapi.pdf]]"],[18055,"[[blenderpythonapi.pdf]]"],[18106,"[[blenderpythonapi.pdf]]"],[18163,"[[blenderpythonapi.pdf]]"],[18244,"[[blenderpythonapi.pdf]]"],[18253,"[[blenderpythonapi.pdf]]"],[18271,"[[blenderpythonapi.pdf]]"],[18304,"[[blenderpythonapi.pdf]]"],[18409,"[[blenderpythonapi.pdf]]"],[18412,"[[blenderpythonapi.pdf]]"],[18430,"[[blenderpythonapi.pdf]]"],[18433,"[[blenderpythonapi.pdf]]"],[18448,"[[blenderpythonapi.pdf]]"],[18481,"[[blenderpythonapi.pdf]]"],[18490,"[[blenderpythonapi.pdf]]"],[18499,"[[blenderpythonapi.pdf]]"],[18508,"[[blenderpythonapi.pdf]]"],[18511,"[[blenderpythonapi.pdf]]"],[18514,"[[blenderpythonapi.pdf]]"],[18517,"[[blenderpythonapi.pdf]]"],[18520,"[[blenderpythonapi.pdf]]"],[18523,"[[blenderpythonapi.pdf]]"],[18526,"[[blenderpythonapi.pdf]]"],[18529,"[[blenderpythonapi.pdf]]"],[19219,"[[blenderpythonapi.pdf]]"],[19909,"[[blenderpythonapi.pdf]]"],[20170,"[[blenderpythonapi.pdf]]"],[20203,"[[blenderpythonapi.pdf]]"],[6887,"[[blenderpythonapi.pdf]]\n\n[[coreblenderdevelopment.pdf]]"],[17938,"[[blenderpythonapi.pdf]]\n\n[[coreblenderdevelopment.pdf]]"],[18007,"[[blenderpythonapi.pdf]]\n\n[[coreblenderdevelopment.pdf]]"],[18424,"[[blenderpythonapi.pdf]]\n\n[[coreblenderdevelopment.pdf]]"],[2583,"[[blenderpythonapi.pdf]]\n[[geometrycreationandimport.pdf]]"],[21600,"[[C++ Clustering Result Modeling]] represents outputs independently of the algorithm that produced them. A base cluster is a container of records, a center cluster adds a representative record, and a subspace cluster adds dimension weights. A partitional clustering is a collection of cluster pointers that can calculate sizes, memberships, labels, and cross-tabulations. A hierarchical result instead uses leaf and internal nodes joined into a tree, with join values and levels preserving merge structure. A membership vector gives a compact record-to-cluster mapping, while typed core fields and an auxiliary heterogeneous map accommodate algorithm-specific results without forcing every algorithm into one oversized result class.\n\nMost algorithms also need an interchangeable proximity operation. [[C++ Clustering Distance Framework]] defines an abstract callable distance interface over two records and implements concrete Minkowski, Euclidean, simple-matching, mixed-type, and Mahalanobis measures. Attribute metadata dispatches type-appropriate component comparisons, and schema checks prevent incomparable records from being combined silently. Algorithms can receive a distance through their argument object rather than constructing one internally. Symmetric pairwise calculations can be cached in a two-key map, and Boost uBLAS supplies vector and matrix operations for covariance-aware distance. This design isolates mathematical variation behind a narrow, testable interface.\n\n[[C++ Clustering Algorithm Framework]] captures the common contract shared by otherwise different methods. An Arguments object holds the dataset, an optional distance strategy, and type-erased named parameters. A Results object holds the membership vector and additional named outputs. The Algorithm base class owns both and exposes a public clustering operation. That operation validates and transfers arguments, invokes the derived computation, clears stale public results, and fetches the new outputs. Protected pure virtual hooks keep clients from calling lifecycle stages out of order. A dummy implementation demonstrates the minimum work needed to add a new algorithm and shows why parameter validation and a top-level exception boundary belong in every executable example.\n\nThe framework becomes concrete in [[C++ Hierarchical Clustering Implementation]]. Lance-Williams agglomerative methods share construction of a symmetric distance matrix, initialization of a forest of singleton clusterings, selection and merging of the nearest pair, and storage of cluster sizes. Derived linkage classes override only the recurrence that updates distances after a merge, allowing single, complete, group-average, weighted-average, centroid, median, and Ward variants to reuse the same control flow. DIANA supplies the divisive counterpart: it chooses a high-diameter cluster, starts a splinter group from the record least compatible with the remainder, and transfers records while the split improves. Tree visitors later extract a flat partition or render the hierarchy."],[21601,"[[C++ Partitional and Fuzzy Clustering]] implements iterative center-based methods while making mutable working state distinct from fixed parameters. K-means initializes centers from seeded records, alternates assignment and center updates, and stops when memberships stabilize or an iteration limit is reached. Repeated executions create fresh objects, vary seeds, and retain the lowest-error result. Fuzzy c-means stores a membership matrix and alternates weighted prototype and membership updates. K-prototypes inherits the K-means control structure but replaces center updating and dataset validation so numeric means and categorical modes can coexist. Shared summaries and command-line options make behavior and experimental settings visible.\n\nFinally, [[C++ Specialized Clustering Implementations]] demonstrates how the same base architecture accommodates substantially different methods. Genetic k-modes represents categorical partitions as chromosomes and applies fitness evaluation, selection, crossover, and mutation. Fuzzy subspace clustering alternates memberships, centers, and per-cluster dimension weights. Gaussian-mixture clustering implements expectation-maximization, adds covariance regularization for singular estimates, and selects the run with the greatest likelihood. Parallel K-means distributes record blocks with Boost MPI, computes local assignments and sums, combines them with collective reductions, broadcasts updated centers, and gathers memberships at the master process. These implementations show that reuse does not require algorithms to share their mathematics; it requires them to share explicit boundaries for input, lifecycle, working state, and output.\n\nAcross the chapter, software structure makes algorithmic experimentation safer. Representations preserve data meaning, strategies isolate mathematical choices, result objects retain method-specific evidence, and template lifecycles prevent partial execution. Tests and portable builds make comparisons repeatable, while the specialized subclasses reveal which variation points actually survive contact with real algorithms. The result is a library in which adding a method means implementing its distinctive logic rather than rebuilding the surrounding machinery."],[87,"[[c80andnetcore30moderncross-platformdevelopment.pdf]]"],[90,"[[c80andnetcore30moderncross-platformdevelopment.pdf]]"],[99,"[[c80andnetcore30moderncross-platformdevelopment.pdf]]"],[102,"[[c80andnetcore30moderncross-platformdevelopment.pdf]]"],[105,"[[c80andnetcore30moderncross-platformdevelopment.pdf]]"],[120,"[[c80andnetcore30moderncross-platformdevelopment.pdf]]"],[297,"[[c80andnetcore30moderncross-platformdevelopment.pdf]]"],[414,"[[c80andnetcore30moderncross-platformdevelopment.pdf]]"],[501,"[[c80andnetcore30moderncross-platformdevelopment.pdf]]"],[1461,"[[c80andnetcore30moderncross-platf